1
00:00:00,142 --> 00:00:03,975
A United States appeals court has described the grandiose

2
00:00:03,975 --> 00:00:08,279
achievements touted by Elizabeth Holmes and her business partner

3
00:00:08,279 --> 00:00:11,507
as, in its words, half-truths and outright lies.

4
00:00:12,205 --> 00:00:15,543
Their company, Theranos, claimed it could run fast,

5
00:00:15,543 --> 00:00:18,161
accurate blood tests from a single drop.

6
00:00:18,161 --> 00:00:21,892
Private investors once valued it at nine billion dollars.

7
00:00:22,522 --> 00:00:25,755
Yet when a jury weighed the evidence against Holmes,

8
00:00:25,755 --> 00:00:28,801
it did something many people still don't realise.

9
00:00:28,801 --> 00:00:31,350
It convicted her of defrauding investors,

10
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:33,899
and acquitted her of defrauding patients.

11
00:00:34,586 --> 00:00:37,501
So what exactly did the court record establish?

12
00:00:37,501 --> 00:00:40,540
And why did the case that sent her to prison turn

13
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:43,952
on investors, not on the people whose blood was tested?

14
00:00:44,903 --> 00:00:48,363
Today we go through the paperwork: the indictment, the

15
00:00:48,363 --> 00:00:52,656
verdicts, the regulators' findings and the appeals court's opinion.

16
00:00:52,656 --> 00:00:55,602
And later, the one decision Theranos made that

17
00:00:55,602 --> 00:00:58,165
the trial judge treated as an admission.

18
00:00:58,872 --> 00:00:59,979
Chapter one.

19
00:00:59,979 --> 00:01:01,270
One tiny drop.

20
00:01:01,983 --> 00:01:08,013
Holmes founded Theranos in 2003 and ran it as chief executive until 2018.

21
00:01:08,013 --> 00:01:11,729
The appeals court opened its opinion with the

22
00:01:11,729 --> 00:01:15,859
vision she sold: one tiny drop changes everything.

23
00:01:16,523 --> 00:01:21,463
The idea was to replace the needle in the vein with a prick of the finger.

24
00:01:21,463 --> 00:01:24,800
Blood would go into tiny tubes called nanotainers,

25
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:28,139
and be tested on Theranos's own miniature machine.

26
00:01:28,777 --> 00:01:31,036
That machine was called the Edison.

27
00:01:31,036 --> 00:01:35,812
Of all the devices Theranos developed under Holmes, the appeals court said

28
00:01:35,812 --> 00:01:39,298
it was the only one ever used to test patient samples.

29
00:01:39,919 --> 00:01:43,775
In 2009, Ramesh 'Sunny' Balwani joined, later

30
00:01:43,775 --> 00:01:47,801
becoming president and chief operating officer.

31
00:01:47,801 --> 00:01:51,314
The court records that he and Holmes were

32
00:01:51,314 --> 00:01:55,169
in a romantic relationship from 2004 to 2016.

33
00:01:55,919 --> 00:02:00,317
Evidence at both trials showed Balwani oversaw the patient-testing

34
00:02:00,317 --> 00:02:03,315
lab and the deals with Safeway and Walgreens.

35
00:02:03,315 --> 00:02:08,511
But Holmes admitted that, as chief executive, ultimately all roads led to her.

36
00:02:09,158 --> 00:02:15,741
In 2010, Theranos partnered with Walgreens to test patients in its pharmacies.

37
00:02:15,741 --> 00:02:19,540
The public launch came in the autumn of 2013.

38
00:02:20,110 --> 00:02:22,538
Now, the number that matters most.

39
00:02:22,538 --> 00:02:26,965
Theranos developed 300 small-sample tests while Holmes ran it.

40
00:02:26,965 --> 00:02:29,821
Only twelve were ever run on the Edison.

41
00:02:30,396 --> 00:02:33,855
Other general chemistry tests ran on machines made

42
00:02:33,855 --> 00:02:37,313
by other manufacturers, some modified by Theranos.

43
00:02:37,313 --> 00:02:41,049
Holmes and Balwani pushed lab staff to move more tests

44
00:02:41,049 --> 00:02:45,130
onto the Edison, despite their concerns about its accuracy.

45
00:02:45,824 --> 00:02:46,828
Chapter two.

46
00:02:46,828 --> 00:02:47,999
The demo room.

47
00:02:48,714 --> 00:02:54,015
Former employees testified at both trials that the Edison consistently failed

48
00:02:54,015 --> 00:02:58,560
quality control checks and was unable to provide accurate results.

49
00:02:59,190 --> 00:03:03,447
According to the court, they told Holmes and Balwani in real time.

50
00:03:03,447 --> 00:03:08,414
Those concerns were dismissed, or the blame was shifted away from the device.

51
00:03:08,999 --> 00:03:13,547
One laboratory director, Dr Mark Pandori, testified that the failure

52
00:03:13,547 --> 00:03:17,159
rate of the controls on the Edisons was notably higher

53
00:03:17,159 --> 00:03:19,634
than on the equipment he was used to.

54
00:03:20,333 --> 00:03:24,860
He also raised proficiency testing, the regulatory check meant to

55
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:27,854
be a neutral way to judge a test's quality.

56
00:03:27,854 --> 00:03:31,615
At Theranos, those checks ran on third-party machines,

57
00:03:31,615 --> 00:03:35,167
while real patient samples ran on Theranos devices.

58
00:03:35,888 --> 00:03:37,780
Then there were the visitors.

59
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:43,520
Employees testified that VIP guests, including investors, were shown into a room with an

60
00:03:43,520 --> 00:03:47,891
Edison or a minilab, and led to believe it was testing their blood.

61
00:03:48,523 --> 00:03:52,853
In reality, the court said, the device was running a null protocol,

62
00:03:52,853 --> 00:03:57,378
while some of those samples were secretly run on third-party machines.

63
00:03:58,015 --> 00:04:02,703
Several investors testified that Holmes and Balwani led them to believe

64
00:04:02,703 --> 00:04:06,401
Theranos ran its tests only on its own devices, and that

65
00:04:06,401 --> 00:04:10,033
the truth would have affected their decision to invest.

66
00:04:10,745 --> 00:04:11,722
Chapter three.

67
00:04:11,722 --> 00:04:13,467
What investors were told.

68
00:04:14,174 --> 00:04:18,371
After the Walgreens launch, Theranos sold new shares.

69
00:04:18,371 --> 00:04:23,123
Three investors bought in late 2013, and three more in 2014.

70
00:04:23,123 --> 00:04:26,608
Each purchase became a separate fraud count.

71
00:04:27,190 --> 00:04:31,700
The indictment grouped the alleged misstatements into seven categories.

72
00:04:32,269 --> 00:04:34,107
Start with the money.

73
00:04:34,107 --> 00:04:41,022
Theranos's corporate controller testified the company had no revenue in 2012 or

74
00:04:41,022 --> 00:04:46,099
2013, and net losses of 57 million and 92 million dollars.

75
00:04:46,745 --> 00:04:51,675
In November 2013, Balwani told Holmes the company was down to 15 million

76
00:04:51,675 --> 00:04:56,125
dollars in cash, while spending up to two million dollars a week.

77
00:04:56,125 --> 00:04:59,822
By our arithmetic, that is about seven weeks of money.

78
00:05:00,459 --> 00:05:02,388
What did investors see?

79
00:05:02,388 --> 00:05:09,182
One, Lisa Peterson, testified that the financial documents she received projected

80
00:05:09,182 --> 00:05:13,459
a profit of 230 million dollars by the end of 2015.

81
00:05:14,110 --> 00:05:19,070
Another, Brian Grossman, testified he was told Theranos had generated

82
00:05:19,070 --> 00:05:23,526
200 million dollars of revenue from the Department of Defense.

83
00:05:24,174 --> 00:05:25,489
And the reality?

84
00:05:25,489 --> 00:05:30,259
In 2015, Theranos reported total income of about one point

85
00:05:30,259 --> 00:05:34,535
nine four million dollars to the US tax authorities.

86
00:05:35,189 --> 00:05:40,881
The Securities and Exchange Commission alleged something similar for 2014:

87
00:05:40,881 --> 00:05:44,882
a claim of more than 100 million dollars in revenue,

88
00:05:44,882 --> 00:05:49,420
against a little more than 100,000 dollars from operations.

89
00:05:50,141 --> 00:05:51,340
Then the military.

90
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:55,002
Theranos did have a relationship with the Department of

91
00:05:55,002 --> 00:05:58,730
Defense, to explore using the Edison in combat settings.

92
00:05:58,730 --> 00:06:01,859
The appeals court said that never materialised.

93
00:06:02,491 --> 00:06:06,819
On a recorded investor call in December 2013, Holmes said

94
00:06:06,819 --> 00:06:10,539
Theranos had been doing a lot of work for special

95
00:06:10,539 --> 00:06:15,322
operations command, in the context of missions in remote areas.

96
00:06:16,046 --> 00:06:20,185
But General James Mattis testified that the device was never put

97
00:06:20,185 --> 00:06:24,130
into the field by the military, never installed on a medevac,

98
00:06:24,130 --> 00:06:27,945
and never used to treat service members on the battlefield.

99
00:06:28,650 --> 00:06:29,814
Then the logos.

100
00:06:29,814 --> 00:06:34,238
A Pfizer representative testified that after 2008, Pfizer

101
00:06:34,238 --> 00:06:38,040
and Theranos had no meaningful business dealings.

102
00:06:38,681 --> 00:06:42,126
Yet Holmes admitted she personally put the logos of

103
00:06:42,126 --> 00:06:47,598
drug companies, including Pfizer, GlaxoSmithKline and Schering-Plough, on reports

104
00:06:47,598 --> 00:06:51,245
with favourable conclusions about the Theranos device.

105
00:06:51,952 --> 00:06:54,923
In emails to Walgreens, she described those

106
00:06:54,923 --> 00:06:58,311
reports as the companies' independent validation.

107
00:06:58,311 --> 00:07:02,319
Their representatives testified they had neither validated

108
00:07:02,319 --> 00:07:05,222
the technology nor authorised their logos.

109
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:07,259
And Walgreens itself?

110
00:07:07,259 --> 00:07:12,039
Its representative testified that while investors heard the partnership was

111
00:07:12,039 --> 00:07:16,182
expanding, it was contracting, partly because so many blood draws

112
00:07:16,182 --> 00:07:18,796
were taken from the vein, not the finger.

113
00:07:19,507 --> 00:07:20,511
Chapter four.

114
00:07:20,511 --> 00:07:22,209
The regulators arrive.

115
00:07:22,936 --> 00:07:28,443
In late 2015, news reporting revealed problems inside the Theranos lab.

116
00:07:28,443 --> 00:07:31,935
Around then, the federal agency that oversees

117
00:07:31,935 --> 00:07:36,511
testing laboratories carried out an unannounced inspection.

118
00:07:37,158 --> 00:07:42,060
That agency, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, concluded that

119
00:07:42,060 --> 00:07:46,702
deficiencies in the lab presented immediate jeopardy to patient health.

120
00:07:47,348 --> 00:07:50,319
In March 2016, Holmes hired a new

121
00:07:50,319 --> 00:07:54,732
laboratory director, Dr Kingshuk Das, to respond.

122
00:07:54,732 --> 00:08:01,846
His team went back through the Edison's quality-control data for 2014 and 2015.

123
00:08:02,523 --> 00:08:07,221
Their written assessment concluded there was a possible patient impact

124
00:08:07,221 --> 00:08:11,115
for every test reported from the lab's Edison instruments.

125
00:08:11,115 --> 00:08:15,276
Das testified he found the device unsuitable for clinical use.

126
00:08:15,919 --> 00:08:18,730
When he raised it, Das said, Holmes blamed

127
00:08:18,730 --> 00:08:22,142
laboratory control failures rather than the device.

128
00:08:22,142 --> 00:08:24,752
He found that explanation unsatisfying.

129
00:08:25,379 --> 00:08:27,779
Then came the decision we promised.

130
00:08:27,779 --> 00:08:32,166
Theranos voided all of its patient test results from the Edison.

131
00:08:32,745 --> 00:08:37,291
Holmes argued it was a precaution that should not be used against her.

132
00:08:37,291 --> 00:08:39,888
The trial judge let the evidence in, and

133
00:08:39,888 --> 00:08:42,940
the appeals court found no abuse of discretion.

134
00:08:43,571 --> 00:08:48,283
The appeals court said the trial judge reasonably concluded that voiding

135
00:08:48,283 --> 00:08:52,603
every Edison result was an admission by Theranos, and by extension

136
00:08:52,603 --> 00:08:56,727
Holmes, that the results were, and always had been, unreliable.

137
00:08:57,443 --> 00:09:02,300
In July 2016, Theranos disclosed the agency's sanctions: its Newark

138
00:09:02,300 --> 00:09:06,796
lab's certificate would be revoked, and Holmes would be barred

139
00:09:06,796 --> 00:09:10,058
from owning or operating a lab for two years.

140
00:09:10,777 --> 00:09:16,100
In March 2018, the Securities and Exchange Commission charged Theranos,

141
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:20,225
Holmes and Balwani with what it called a massive fraud,

142
00:09:20,225 --> 00:09:24,199
raising more than 700 million dollars from investors.

143
00:09:24,936 --> 00:09:29,155
Holmes settled without admitting or denying the allegations.

144
00:09:29,155 --> 00:09:32,952
She agreed to a 500,000 dollar penalty, a ten-year bar

145
00:09:32,952 --> 00:09:37,101
as a public company officer or director, to return eighteen

146
00:09:37,101 --> 00:09:41,110
point nine million shares, and to give up voting control.

147
00:09:41,824 --> 00:09:42,843
Chapter five.

148
00:09:42,843 --> 00:09:44,567
What the jury decided.

149
00:09:45,285 --> 00:09:48,499
After a two and a half year investigation,

150
00:09:48,499 --> 00:09:51,638
a grand jury indicted Holmes and Balwani.

151
00:09:51,638 --> 00:09:54,393
The final indictment had two halves.

152
00:09:54,393 --> 00:09:59,061
The first alleged a conspiracy against investors from 2010 to

153
00:09:59,061 --> 00:10:03,194
2015, plus six counts of wire fraud, one per investor.

154
00:10:03,919 --> 00:10:09,428
The second alleged a conspiracy against patients from 2013 to

155
00:10:09,428 --> 00:10:14,395
2016, and four counts of wire fraud involving patients.

156
00:10:15,062 --> 00:10:16,422
Patients testified.

157
00:10:16,422 --> 00:10:19,858
One said a Theranos HIV test came back positive,

158
00:10:19,858 --> 00:10:23,006
but two later tests showed she was negative.

159
00:10:23,006 --> 00:10:27,730
Another said she received an incorrect result about her pregnancy.

160
00:10:28,364 --> 00:10:32,295
The court tried the two separately, after Holmes made

161
00:10:32,295 --> 00:10:36,968
allegations of abuse by Balwani in their personal relationship.

162
00:10:36,968 --> 00:10:39,341
Holmes went first, in late 2021.

163
00:10:39,341 --> 00:10:42,085
Each trial lasted nearly four months.

164
00:10:42,745 --> 00:10:46,436
On the third of January 2022, after seven days

165
00:10:46,436 --> 00:10:50,528
of deliberation, the jury returned a split verdict.

166
00:10:51,190 --> 00:10:55,440
Guilty: the investor conspiracy, and wire fraud against

167
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:58,996
the three investors who bought shares in 2014.

168
00:10:59,634 --> 00:11:04,866
Not guilty: the patient conspiracy, and the three remaining patient counts.

169
00:11:04,866 --> 00:11:07,308
Prosecutors had dropped the fourth.

170
00:11:07,308 --> 00:11:11,702
And on the three late-2013 investors, the jury could not agree.

171
00:11:12,269 --> 00:11:16,219
In a separate trial in 2022, another jury convicted

172
00:11:16,219 --> 00:11:20,867
Balwani on all twelve counts, including every patient count.

173
00:11:21,507 --> 00:11:22,748
Why the difference?

174
00:11:22,748 --> 00:11:27,385
A verdict doesn't give reasons, and we won't guess what jurors thought.

175
00:11:27,385 --> 00:11:32,154
The appeals court does record that evidence linked Balwani to sending the

176
00:11:32,154 --> 00:11:37,182
inaccurate results and to buying adverts for Theranos clinics in Arizona, and

177
00:11:37,182 --> 00:11:41,755
that Holmes's December 2013 investor call was not played at his trial.

178
00:11:42,459 --> 00:11:43,459
Chapter six.

179
00:11:43,459 --> 00:11:44,959
Prison and appeal.

180
00:11:45,666 --> 00:11:49,518
At sentencing, the judge found a loss of

181
00:11:49,518 --> 00:11:53,754
120 million dollars and ten or more victims.

182
00:11:53,754 --> 00:11:58,857
That produced a guideline range of 135 to 168 months.

183
00:11:59,539 --> 00:12:05,980
On the eighteenth of November 2022, Holmes was sentenced to 135 months.

184
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:08,702
Eleven years and three months.

185
00:12:09,285 --> 00:12:13,511
In court, according to CNBC, Holmes told the judge she was

186
00:12:13,511 --> 00:12:18,538
devastated by her failings, and apologised to investors and patients.

187
00:12:19,190 --> 00:12:21,873
Balwani received 155 months.

188
00:12:21,873 --> 00:12:25,801
In May 2023, the court ordered the two of

189
00:12:25,801 --> 00:12:31,264
them, jointly, to pay 452 million dollars in restitution.

190
00:12:31,919 --> 00:12:37,128
That included 397 million dollars for twelve investors, and fifty four

191
00:12:37,128 --> 00:12:42,187
and a half million for two corporate victims: Safeway and Walgreens.

192
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:43,863
Both appealed.

193
00:12:43,863 --> 00:12:48,615
On the twenty-fourth of February 2025, the Ninth Circuit Court of

194
00:12:48,615 --> 00:12:54,023
Appeals affirmed the convictions, the sentences and the restitution order.

195
00:12:54,681 --> 00:12:59,616
It did accept part of one complaint: some testimony from three former lab

196
00:12:59,616 --> 00:13:05,159
directors amounted to expert opinion, without the usual checks on expert evidence.

197
00:13:05,793 --> 00:13:07,916
But it called the error harmless.

198
00:13:07,916 --> 00:13:11,067
All three would easily have qualified as experts.

199
00:13:11,067 --> 00:13:14,283
And for Dr Das, other employees described the same

200
00:13:14,283 --> 00:13:17,436
problems, and the technology was only one of many

201
00:13:17,436 --> 00:13:21,552
kinds of misrepresentation the government presented evidence of.

202
00:13:22,238 --> 00:13:24,688
It also said the trial judge should have

203
00:13:24,688 --> 00:13:27,444
considered whether the shares kept any value.

204
00:13:27,444 --> 00:13:30,753
Again, harmless: the investors could not sell them, so

205
00:13:30,753 --> 00:13:34,122
for restitution, their loss was everything they put in.

206
00:13:34,809 --> 00:13:35,988
One more twist.

207
00:13:35,988 --> 00:13:39,999
In March 2026, Holmes's sentence was cut by a year,

208
00:13:39,999 --> 00:13:44,402
under a 2023 change for people with no criminal history.

209
00:13:44,402 --> 00:13:49,907
The judge wrote that this did not diminish the enormity of her crimes.

210
00:13:50,555 --> 00:13:51,735
Chapter seven.

211
00:13:51,735 --> 00:13:52,830
Our analysis.

212
00:13:53,539 --> 00:13:56,138
So what did the jury actually decide?

213
00:13:56,138 --> 00:13:59,579
It did not convict Holmes of defrauding patients.

214
00:13:59,579 --> 00:14:04,636
It convicted her of defrauding investors, specifically the three who put

215
00:14:04,636 --> 00:14:08,077
money in during 2014, and of conspiring to do so.

216
00:14:08,714 --> 00:14:09,568
That matters.

217
00:14:09,568 --> 00:14:13,907
The popular story of Theranos is about a machine that didn't work.

218
00:14:13,907 --> 00:14:16,142
The legal story, as we read it, is

219
00:14:16,142 --> 00:14:19,233
narrower: it is about what investors were told.

220
00:14:19,857 --> 00:14:23,877
Look at the list of misstatements the appeals court described:

221
00:14:23,877 --> 00:14:29,259
finances, third-party machines, Walgreens, the military and the drug-company logos.

222
00:14:29,259 --> 00:14:32,956
It said the lab director's testimony about the technology

223
00:14:32,956 --> 00:14:35,615
was not essential to proving any of them.

224
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:38,386
In other words, you didn't need to

225
00:14:38,386 --> 00:14:41,207
understand blood chemistry to follow the case.

226
00:14:41,207 --> 00:14:46,053
You needed to compare what investors were shown with the company's own records.

227
00:14:46,681 --> 00:14:50,739
Two hundred and thirty million dollars of projected profit,

228
00:14:50,739 --> 00:14:54,383
against under two million dollars of reported income.

229
00:14:54,383 --> 00:14:57,959
A military that never put the device into the field.

230
00:14:57,959 --> 00:15:00,917
Logos added by the chief executive herself.

231
00:15:01,571 --> 00:15:05,017
Our view: the patient side shows how hard it can be to

232
00:15:05,017 --> 00:15:09,611
turn bad test results into a fraud conviction against a chief executive.

233
00:15:09,611 --> 00:15:14,652
The investor side shows what becomes provable when the claims are written down.

234
00:15:15,285 --> 00:15:18,495
For founders, the lesson is not to stop dreaming.

235
00:15:18,495 --> 00:15:21,705
It is that every projection, every logo and every

236
00:15:21,705 --> 00:15:24,850
demo shown to an investor can become an exhibit.

237
00:15:25,476 --> 00:15:28,659
And for the rest of us, it is a reminder to ask the

238
00:15:28,659 --> 00:15:33,966
simplest question of any miracle machine: whose machine is actually running the test?

239
00:15:34,936 --> 00:15:40,013
Every fact in this episode comes from the court record, the regulators and the

240
00:15:40,013 --> 00:15:44,765
reporting listed in the description, with each source on screen as we go.

241
00:15:45,412 --> 00:15:49,435
If you want more collapses told from the paperwork, subscribe.

242
00:15:49,435 --> 00:15:51,771
Next week: the run on Northern Rock.
